1
00:00:01,600 --> 00:00:06,102
Every force problem in IB Physics comes
down to two moves: draw the forces,

2
00:00:06,142 --> 00:00:08,080
then split them into components.

3
00:00:08,262 --> 00:00:11,362
So where does mg sin θ actually come from?

4
00:00:11,544 --> 00:00:12,870
Most people memorise it,

5
00:00:12,910 --> 00:00:16,098
and then put the sine and the
cosine on the wrong sides.

6
00:00:16,280 --> 00:00:19,469
We're going to build it instead,
so you never have to guess.

7
00:00:20,009 --> 00:00:24,073
In the next few minutes we'll
derive every contact force you need:

8
00:00:24,113 --> 00:00:28,031
the normal force, friction,
tension, buoyancy and viscous drag.

9
00:00:28,213 --> 00:00:31,475
No springs, no circles, no kinematics.

10
00:00:31,657 --> 00:00:35,867
Just forces, triangles, and the
trigonometry that connects them.

11
00:00:36,767 --> 00:00:39,504
First, the one tool
we'll use over and over.

12
00:00:39,685 --> 00:00:42,542
A force is an arrow with
a size and a direction,

13
00:00:42,582 --> 00:00:47,171
and any arrow can be replaced by two
perpendicular arrows that add up to it.

14
00:00:47,353 --> 00:00:49,347
Splitting doesn't change the force.

15
00:00:49,529 --> 00:00:52,300
It just lets us deal with
one direction at a time.

16
00:00:52,844 --> 00:00:56,631
Make the force F the hypotenuse
of a right-angled triangle,

17
00:00:56,671 --> 00:00:58,552
at angle θ to the horizontal.

18
00:00:58,734 --> 00:01:04,244
Cosine is adjacent over hypotenuse, so
the side touching the angle is F cos θ.

19
00:01:04,426 --> 00:01:09,668
Sine is opposite over hypotenuse, so the
side across from the angle is F sin θ.

20
00:01:10,209 --> 00:01:14,808
Now swing the angle and watch: as one
component grows, the other shrinks,

21
00:01:14,848 --> 00:01:17,072
but together they always rebuild F.

22
00:01:17,609 --> 00:01:21,038
The rule to remember: cos
goes with the side touching θ,

23
00:01:21,078 --> 00:01:23,432
sin goes with the side across from it.

24
00:01:23,614 --> 00:01:25,033
Try it with numbers.

25
00:01:25,215 --> 00:01:27,753
A ten newton force at thirty degrees has

26
00:01:27,793 --> 00:01:30,784
eight point seven newtons
along the horizontal,

27
00:01:30,824 --> 00:01:32,887
and exactly five newtons across.

28
00:01:33,800 --> 00:01:36,609
Start simple: a block resting on a table.

29
00:01:36,791 --> 00:01:39,668
Its weight, mg, pulls straight down.

30
00:01:39,850 --> 00:01:42,504
The table pushes back up
with the normal force,

31
00:01:42,544 --> 00:01:45,067
normal meaning perpendicular
to the surface.

32
00:01:45,604 --> 00:01:48,921
Nothing accelerates, so the
vertical forces balance,

33
00:01:48,961 --> 00:01:50,963
and the normal force equals mg.

34
00:01:51,505 --> 00:01:54,061
But these two are not a
Newton's third law pair.

35
00:01:54,242 --> 00:01:55,668
Both act on the block.

36
00:01:55,850 --> 00:02:00,213
A third law pair is the same kind of
force acting on two different objects.

37
00:02:00,395 --> 00:02:03,574
The partner of the weight is the
block pulling the Earth upward.

38
00:02:04,114 --> 00:02:08,095
Push down on the block with your hand,
and the table pushes back harder.

39
00:02:08,276 --> 00:02:12,434
The normal force isn't fixed: it
responds to whatever it has to hold up.

40
00:02:12,616 --> 00:02:16,687
It only equals mg here because
nothing else is pushing up or down.

41
00:02:17,600 --> 00:02:21,284
Now pull the block with a rope
at angle θ above the horizontal.

42
00:02:21,466 --> 00:02:27,760
Split the pull: F cos θ drags the block
sideways, and F sin θ lifts it a little.

43
00:02:28,303 --> 00:02:33,943
Vertically, everything still balances:
the normal force plus F sin θ equals mg.

44
00:02:34,125 --> 00:02:37,608
So the normal force is mg minus F sin θ.

45
00:02:37,790 --> 00:02:40,060
Pull harder and the
surface pushes back less,

46
00:02:40,100 --> 00:02:42,564
because the rope is
carrying part of the weight.

47
00:02:42,746 --> 00:02:47,019
Pull hard enough and the normal force
reaches zero: the block lifts off.

48
00:02:47,556 --> 00:02:52,647
Push down at the same angle instead, and
the vertical part adds to the weight:

49
00:02:52,687 --> 00:02:55,384
the normal force becomes mg plus F sin θ.

50
00:02:55,566 --> 00:03:00,041
Since friction is proportional to the
normal force, pulling up reduces friction,

51
00:03:00,081 --> 00:03:01,774
and pushing down increases it.

52
00:03:02,700 --> 00:03:03,995
Let's put numbers on it.

53
00:03:04,177 --> 00:03:07,543
A ten kilogram crate is pulled
along the floor by a rope,

54
00:03:07,583 --> 00:03:11,108
with thirty newtons at thirty
degrees above the horizontal.

55
00:03:11,289 --> 00:03:13,776
The coefficient of friction is point two.

56
00:03:14,315 --> 00:03:19,545
Vertically, the normal force is mg
minus F sin θ: ninety eight point one,

57
00:03:19,585 --> 00:03:23,123
minus fifteen, is eighty
three point one newtons.

58
00:03:23,661 --> 00:03:27,174
So the friction is point two
times eighty three point one:

59
00:03:27,214 --> 00:03:28,807
sixteen point six newtons.

60
00:03:29,349 --> 00:03:33,655
The rope pulls forward with
F cos θ: twenty six newtons.

61
00:03:33,837 --> 00:03:38,753
The resultant is twenty six minus sixteen
point six: nine point four newtons,

62
00:03:38,793 --> 00:03:42,228
so the acceleration is nine
point four divided by ten:

63
00:03:42,268 --> 00:03:44,971
point nine four metres per second squared.

64
00:03:45,867 --> 00:03:49,461
The normal force is also what you
feel through your feet in a lift.

65
00:03:49,643 --> 00:03:51,053
Take up as positive.

66
00:03:51,235 --> 00:03:55,154
Accelerating upward, the floor
must push harder than your weight.

67
00:03:55,335 --> 00:03:59,917
Newton's second law gives the
normal force minus mg equals ma,

68
00:03:59,957 --> 00:04:02,939
so the normal force is m times g plus a.

69
00:04:03,481 --> 00:04:07,131
Accelerating downward,
it's m times g minus a.

70
00:04:07,313 --> 00:04:12,630
At constant speed there's no acceleration,
and the floor pushes with exactly mg.

71
00:04:12,812 --> 00:04:14,478
Your weight never changes.

72
00:04:14,659 --> 00:04:18,179
Only the contact force does,
and that's what the scales read.

73
00:04:18,361 --> 00:04:22,952
If the cable snapped, a would equal
g, and the normal force would be zero.

74
00:04:23,133 --> 00:04:25,352
That's what feeling
weightless really means.

75
00:04:26,267 --> 00:04:30,242
A seventy kilogram person stands
on a set of scales in a lift.

76
00:04:30,782 --> 00:04:33,533
At constant speed, the
scales read the weight:

77
00:04:33,573 --> 00:04:38,125
seventy times nine point eight one is
six hundred and eighty seven newtons.

78
00:04:38,664 --> 00:04:42,581
Accelerating upward at one point
two metres per second squared,

79
00:04:42,621 --> 00:04:47,292
the normal force is seventy times, nine
point eight one plus one point two:

80
00:04:47,332 --> 00:04:49,719
seven hundred and seventy one newtons.

81
00:04:50,259 --> 00:04:53,997
Accelerating downward at the
same rate, it's seventy times,

82
00:04:54,037 --> 00:04:58,648
nine point eight one minus one point
two: six hundred and three newtons.

83
00:04:58,830 --> 00:05:00,800
The person's weight never changed.

84
00:05:00,982 --> 00:05:02,310
Only the reading did.

85
00:05:03,200 --> 00:05:06,449
Now the classic: a block
on a slope at angle θ.

86
00:05:06,631 --> 00:05:10,668
Gravity doesn't care about the slope, so
the weight still points straight down.

87
00:05:10,850 --> 00:05:14,060
But the slope can only push
perpendicular to itself.

88
00:05:14,600 --> 00:05:18,659
So tilt your point of view to
match the slope: one axis along it,

89
00:05:18,699 --> 00:05:20,213
one perpendicular to it.

90
00:05:20,394 --> 00:05:23,889
That way the normal force
lands exactly on one axis.

91
00:05:24,071 --> 00:05:27,519
Now the weight is the only force
that isn't lined up with an axis,

92
00:05:27,559 --> 00:05:28,880
so it's the one we split.

93
00:05:29,420 --> 00:05:30,966
Here's the key geometry.

94
00:05:31,148 --> 00:05:34,773
Start with the slope flat: its
perpendicular points straight down,

95
00:05:34,813 --> 00:05:36,090
right along the weight.

96
00:05:36,272 --> 00:05:38,122
Now tilt the slope by θ.

97
00:05:38,304 --> 00:05:41,451
Its perpendicular tilts
by exactly the same θ.

98
00:05:41,632 --> 00:05:45,145
So the angle between the weight
and the perpendicular is θ.

99
00:05:45,689 --> 00:05:46,896
Read the triangle.

100
00:05:47,078 --> 00:05:51,996
The component touching θ, pressing
into the slope, is mg cos θ.

101
00:05:52,178 --> 00:05:56,961
The component across from θ,
pulling down the slope, is mg sin θ.

102
00:05:57,143 --> 00:06:01,819
Nothing moves into the slope, so
the normal force equals mg cos θ.

103
00:06:02,364 --> 00:06:03,684
A quick example.

104
00:06:03,866 --> 00:06:06,693
A two kilogram block on
a thirty degree slope:

105
00:06:06,733 --> 00:06:11,346
down the slope is nine point eight
newtons, and into the slope, seventeen.

106
00:06:11,888 --> 00:06:13,262
Check the extremes.

107
00:06:13,444 --> 00:06:18,048
At zero degrees, sine is zero and
cosine is one: no pull along the slope,

108
00:06:18,088 --> 00:06:20,314
and the full weight on the surface.

109
00:06:20,495 --> 00:06:24,519
At ninety degrees the slope is a
wall: all of the weight acts along it,

110
00:06:24,559 --> 00:06:25,990
and none presses into it.

111
00:06:26,171 --> 00:06:30,051
So if you ever forget which is
sine, tilt the slope almost flat,

112
00:06:30,091 --> 00:06:32,235
and ask which part should be small.

113
00:06:33,133 --> 00:06:34,090
Add friction.

114
00:06:34,271 --> 00:06:36,911
Push a heavy box gently
and it doesn't move,

115
00:06:36,951 --> 00:06:40,423
because friction pushes back
exactly as hard as you push.

116
00:06:40,605 --> 00:06:44,619
Push harder, and friction
rises to match, up to a limit:

117
00:06:44,659 --> 00:06:46,541
μs times the normal force.

118
00:06:46,722 --> 00:06:52,290
On the slope, static friction exactly
matches the down-slope pull, mg sin θ.

119
00:06:52,834 --> 00:06:53,963
Raise the angle.

120
00:06:54,145 --> 00:07:01,311
mg sin θ grows, while the maximum
friction, μs mg cos θ, shrinks.

121
00:07:01,493 --> 00:07:03,277
They meet at the tipping point.

122
00:07:03,825 --> 00:07:09,389
Set them equal: mg sin
θ equals μs mg cos θ.

123
00:07:09,571 --> 00:07:13,930
The mass cancels, divide by
cos θ, and μs equals tan θ,

124
00:07:13,970 --> 00:07:16,848
because sine over cosine is tangent.

125
00:07:17,030 --> 00:07:21,551
Measure the angle where a block just
starts to slip, and you've measured μs.

126
00:07:22,091 --> 00:07:25,749
Once it slides, friction drops
to μd times the normal force,

127
00:07:25,789 --> 00:07:29,178
because moving surfaces grip
less than stationary ones.

128
00:07:29,360 --> 00:07:35,738
So the resultant force down the
slope is mg sin θ minus μd mg cos θ.

129
00:07:36,633 --> 00:07:39,662
Tilt a board until a block
just starts to slide.

130
00:07:39,843 --> 00:07:41,944
It slips at twenty eight degrees.

131
00:07:42,126 --> 00:07:46,086
Then the coefficient of static
friction is tan twenty eight degrees:

132
00:07:46,126 --> 00:07:47,126
point five three.

133
00:07:47,671 --> 00:07:50,836
Now a five kilogram block on
a thirty five degree slope,

134
00:07:50,876 --> 00:07:53,794
with a sliding friction
coefficient of point three.

135
00:07:53,976 --> 00:07:55,012
Down the slope,

136
00:07:55,052 --> 00:08:00,174
mg sin θ is five times nine point
eight one times point five seven four:

137
00:08:00,214 --> 00:08:02,436
twenty eight point one newtons.

138
00:08:02,618 --> 00:08:04,307
Friction, μd mg cos θ,

139
00:08:04,347 --> 00:08:10,593
is point three times five times nine point
eight one times point eight one nine:

140
00:08:10,633 --> 00:08:11,811
twelve newtons.

141
00:08:12,352 --> 00:08:15,671
The resultant is sixteen point
one newtons down the slope,

142
00:08:15,711 --> 00:08:19,474
so the acceleration is three point
two metres per second squared.

143
00:08:20,400 --> 00:08:21,624
Here's a trickier one.

144
00:08:21,805 --> 00:08:25,328
Hold a block on a smooth slope
with a horizontal push F.

145
00:08:25,510 --> 00:08:29,451
Now F isn't lined up with either
axis, so it gets split too.

146
00:08:29,990 --> 00:08:32,817
The angle between F and
the slope is also θ,

147
00:08:32,857 --> 00:08:37,613
because F is horizontal and the slope
is tilted by θ from the horizontal.

148
00:08:37,795 --> 00:08:42,329
F is the hypotenuse, so F cos
θ pushes up along the slope,

149
00:08:42,369 --> 00:08:44,577
and F sin θ presses into it.

150
00:08:45,119 --> 00:08:51,165
Along the slope, F cos θ balances
mg sin θ, so F equals mg tan θ,

151
00:08:51,205 --> 00:08:54,575
because sine over cosine is tangent.

152
00:08:54,757 --> 00:08:59,618
Perpendicular to the slope, the
normal force must balance both:

153
00:08:59,658 --> 00:09:01,369
mg cos θ plus F sin θ.

154
00:09:01,551 --> 00:09:05,515
The push adds to the normal force, so it
can even end up bigger than the weight.

155
00:09:06,433 --> 00:09:11,597
A ten kilogram block is held on a smooth
ramp, tilted at twenty five degrees,

156
00:09:11,637 --> 00:09:13,056
by a horizontal push.

157
00:09:13,593 --> 00:09:20,245
Along the slope, F cos θ equals
mg sin θ, so F equals mg tan θ.

158
00:09:20,427 --> 00:09:24,544
That's ninety eight point one
times zero point four six six:

159
00:09:24,584 --> 00:09:26,731
forty five point seven newtons.

160
00:09:27,275 --> 00:09:32,850
Perpendicular to the slope, the
normal force is mg cos θ plus F sin θ.

161
00:09:33,032 --> 00:09:36,754
That's eighty eight point nine,
plus nineteen point three:

162
00:09:36,794 --> 00:09:38,739
one hundred and eight newtons.

163
00:09:38,920 --> 00:09:39,914
Bigger than the weight.

164
00:09:40,833 --> 00:09:42,642
Tension is the pull in a string,

165
00:09:42,682 --> 00:09:45,628
directed along the string
and away from the object.

166
00:09:45,810 --> 00:09:48,504
A string can only pull, never push.

167
00:09:48,686 --> 00:09:52,588
Hang a mass from a single string
and the tension equals mg.

168
00:09:53,135 --> 00:09:57,482
Now hang a lamp from two strings,
each at angle α to the horizontal.

169
00:09:57,664 --> 00:10:02,910
Split each tension: T cos α
sideways, and T sin α upward.

170
00:10:03,092 --> 00:10:04,781
The sideways parts cancel.

171
00:10:04,962 --> 00:10:09,648
The upward parts share the
weight: two T sin α equals mg,

172
00:10:09,688 --> 00:10:12,175
so T equals mg over two sin α.

173
00:10:12,720 --> 00:10:17,191
Flatten the strings and watch: as
α shrinks, sin α heads toward zero,

174
00:10:17,231 --> 00:10:18,930
and the tension shoots up.

175
00:10:19,112 --> 00:10:24,136
At thirty degrees each string carries
exactly mg, and at five degrees,

176
00:10:24,176 --> 00:10:25,622
nearly six times mg.

177
00:10:25,804 --> 00:10:28,997
That's why you can never pull a
washing line perfectly straight.

178
00:10:29,538 --> 00:10:34,875
With unequal angles, balance each
direction separately: sideways,

179
00:10:34,915 --> 00:10:38,763
T one cos α one equals
T two cos α two; upward,

180
00:10:38,803 --> 00:10:42,691
T one sin α one plus T
two sin α two equals mg.

181
00:10:42,873 --> 00:10:45,628
Two equations, two unknown tensions.

182
00:10:45,810 --> 00:10:49,313
Or slide the three forces
nose to tail: in equilibrium,

183
00:10:49,353 --> 00:10:51,092
they close into a triangle.

184
00:10:52,000 --> 00:10:54,663
A ten kilogram sign hangs from two cables,

185
00:10:54,703 --> 00:10:58,565
one at thirty degrees to the
horizontal, the other at sixty.

186
00:10:59,108 --> 00:11:02,861
Sideways, T one cos thirty
equals T two cos sixty,

187
00:11:02,901 --> 00:11:06,391
so T two is one point
seven three times T one.

188
00:11:06,938 --> 00:11:11,514
Upward, T one sin thirty plus
T two sin sixty equals mg.

189
00:11:11,696 --> 00:11:15,595
Put T two in, and the left
side becomes two times T one.

190
00:11:15,777 --> 00:11:20,241
So T one is half of ninety eight
point one: forty nine newtons.

191
00:11:20,782 --> 00:11:25,373
And T two is one point seven three
times that: eighty five newtons.

192
00:11:25,555 --> 00:11:28,096
The steeper cable
carries more of the load.

193
00:11:29,000 --> 00:11:34,422
Over a light, smooth pulley, tension
keeps its size and only changes direction.

194
00:11:34,604 --> 00:11:37,192
Tie a block on a table to a hanging mass.

195
00:11:37,374 --> 00:11:41,258
The same tension pulls the block
sideways and holds the hanging mass up.

196
00:11:41,801 --> 00:11:45,064
Call the hanging mass little
m, and the block big M.

197
00:11:45,246 --> 00:11:49,257
For the hanging mass,
mg minus T equals ma.

198
00:11:49,438 --> 00:11:52,064
For the block, T equals M a.

199
00:11:52,246 --> 00:11:55,556
The string links them, so they
share the same acceleration.

200
00:11:56,101 --> 00:12:02,023
Add the two equations and the tension
cancels: mg equals big M plus little m,

201
00:12:02,063 --> 00:12:02,824
times a.

202
00:12:02,864 --> 00:12:07,211
So the acceleration equals
mg over big M plus little m.

203
00:12:07,393 --> 00:12:10,464
Substitute that back into
the block's equation,

204
00:12:10,504 --> 00:12:15,006
and T equals big M times little m
times g, over big M plus little m.

205
00:12:15,188 --> 00:12:19,295
And while it accelerates, the tension
is always less than the hanging weight,

206
00:12:19,335 --> 00:12:21,705
because the block has
to be accelerated too.

207
00:12:22,600 --> 00:12:25,537
Take a three kilogram
block on a smooth table,

208
00:12:25,577 --> 00:12:28,812
tied over a pulley to a
two kilogram hanging mass.

209
00:12:29,354 --> 00:12:33,605
The acceleration is the hanging
weight divided by the total mass:

210
00:12:33,645 --> 00:12:35,890
nineteen point six two, over five.

211
00:12:36,072 --> 00:12:39,178
That's three point nine two
metres per second squared.

212
00:12:39,721 --> 00:12:43,060
The tension is the block's
mass times the acceleration:

213
00:12:43,100 --> 00:12:47,401
three times three point nine two,
which is eleven point eight newtons.

214
00:12:47,944 --> 00:12:49,389
Check the hanging mass.

215
00:12:49,570 --> 00:12:53,970
Its weight, nineteen point six, minus
the tension, eleven point eight,

216
00:12:54,010 --> 00:12:56,380
leaves seven point eight five newtons,

217
00:12:56,420 --> 00:13:00,289
and two times three point nine
two is seven point eight five.

218
00:13:00,471 --> 00:13:01,334
It works.

219
00:13:02,233 --> 00:13:03,940
Now put a block in a fluid.

220
00:13:04,122 --> 00:13:05,921
Fluid pressure grows with depth,

221
00:13:05,961 --> 00:13:10,097
so the bottom face is pushed up harder
than the top face is pushed down.

222
00:13:10,279 --> 00:13:12,147
The pushes on the sides cancel.

223
00:13:12,328 --> 00:13:13,952
The difference is buoyancy.

224
00:13:14,498 --> 00:13:18,263
The extra pressure across the
block's height h is ρ g h.

225
00:13:18,445 --> 00:13:21,832
That's the weight of a column
of fluid, per unit area.

226
00:13:22,014 --> 00:13:27,173
It acts on the face area A, so
the buoyant force is ρ g h A,

227
00:13:27,213 --> 00:13:31,371
and since h times A is
the volume, that's ρ V g.

228
00:13:31,553 --> 00:13:34,577
Here ρ is the fluid's
density, not the block's,

229
00:13:34,617 --> 00:13:37,354
and V is the volume of fluid pushed aside.

230
00:13:37,536 --> 00:13:40,225
Picture that space filled
with fluid instead.

231
00:13:40,407 --> 00:13:43,813
It would just sit there, so the
fluid around it must be pushing up

232
00:13:43,853 --> 00:13:46,515
with exactly the weight of
the fluid that fills it.

233
00:13:47,057 --> 00:13:51,916
A floating object sinks until it
pushes aside its own weight of fluid:

234
00:13:51,956 --> 00:13:55,245
ρ times the submerged
volume times g equals mg.

235
00:13:56,167 --> 00:14:00,870
A wooden block has a mass of point eight
kilograms and a volume of one litre:

236
00:14:00,910 --> 00:14:02,882
one thousandth of a cubic metre.

237
00:14:03,064 --> 00:14:04,522
Will it float in water?

238
00:14:05,066 --> 00:14:09,014
Fully submerged, the buoyant
force is rho V g: a thousand,

239
00:14:09,054 --> 00:14:12,768
times point zero zero one,
times nine point eight one.

240
00:14:12,950 --> 00:14:15,197
That's nine point eight one newtons.

241
00:14:15,379 --> 00:14:18,302
The weight is only seven
point eight five newtons.

242
00:14:18,484 --> 00:14:21,771
So the buoyant force
wins, and the block rises.

243
00:14:22,309 --> 00:14:25,263
It floats when the buoyant
force equals the weight.

244
00:14:25,445 --> 00:14:30,257
That needs a submerged volume of point
eight kilograms divided by a thousand.

245
00:14:30,439 --> 00:14:33,190
So eighty percent of the
block sits under water.

246
00:14:34,100 --> 00:14:36,641
Move through a fluid, and it drags back.

247
00:14:36,823 --> 00:14:41,641
For a small sphere moving slowly
through smooth, layered flow,

248
00:14:41,681 --> 00:14:44,815
Stokes' law gives the drag: six π η r v.

249
00:14:44,996 --> 00:14:50,264
Here η is the viscosity, how thick and
sticky the fluid is, r is the radius,

250
00:14:50,304 --> 00:14:51,631
and v is the speed.

251
00:14:51,813 --> 00:14:53,175
It grows with speed.

252
00:14:53,717 --> 00:14:55,515
Drop a ball bearing into oil.

253
00:14:55,697 --> 00:14:58,922
Weight pulls down;
buoyancy and drag push up.

254
00:14:59,104 --> 00:15:01,032
As it speeds up, the drag grows,

255
00:15:01,072 --> 00:15:04,394
until the forces balance and
the speed stops changing.

256
00:15:04,576 --> 00:15:05,922
That's terminal speed.

257
00:15:06,466 --> 00:15:07,957
Write the balance:

258
00:15:07,997 --> 00:15:13,780
mg equals the buoyant force plus
six π η r times the terminal speed.

259
00:15:13,962 --> 00:15:18,885
Replace each mass with density times
four thirds π r cubed, and solve.

260
00:15:19,067 --> 00:15:24,385
Move the buoyant force across, cancel
π and one r, and divide by six η.

261
00:15:24,567 --> 00:15:27,426
Four thirds divided by six is two ninths.

262
00:15:27,608 --> 00:15:32,646
The terminal speed is two r squared
g, times the density difference,

263
00:15:32,686 --> 00:15:33,582
over nine η.

264
00:15:34,124 --> 00:15:35,525
Read it like a recipe.

265
00:15:35,707 --> 00:15:39,006
Double the radius and the
ball falls four times faster.

266
00:15:39,188 --> 00:15:42,635
Use oil twice as thick
and it falls half as fast.

267
00:15:43,533 --> 00:15:47,678
Drop a steel ball bearing, two
millimeters in radius, into oil.

268
00:15:47,860 --> 00:15:52,367
The steel's density is seven thousand
eight hundred kilograms per cubic metre.

269
00:15:52,549 --> 00:15:57,378
The oil's is nine hundred, and its
viscosity is point nine pascal seconds.

270
00:15:57,918 --> 00:16:02,956
The terminal speed is two r squared
g, times the density difference,

271
00:16:02,996 --> 00:16:03,892
over nine η.

272
00:16:04,074 --> 00:16:07,781
The radius squared is four
times ten to the minus six.

273
00:16:07,963 --> 00:16:11,075
The density difference is
six thousand nine hundred.

274
00:16:11,618 --> 00:16:16,812
So two, times four times ten to the
minus six, times nine point eight one,

275
00:16:16,852 --> 00:16:21,480
times six thousand nine hundred,
divided by nine times point nine:

276
00:16:21,520 --> 00:16:24,278
point zero six seven metres per second.

277
00:16:24,460 --> 00:16:26,684
About seven centimetres per second.

278
00:16:27,600 --> 00:16:30,494
Every contact force
follows the same recipe.

279
00:16:30,676 --> 00:16:34,164
Isolate the object, and draw
only the forces acting on it.

280
00:16:34,346 --> 00:16:36,606
Choose axes that match the surface.

281
00:16:36,787 --> 00:16:41,296
Split each force with its triangle:
cos for the side touching the angle,

282
00:16:41,336 --> 00:16:42,853
sin for the side across.

283
00:16:43,035 --> 00:16:44,735
Then balance each direction.

284
00:16:45,281 --> 00:16:46,728
Three slips to avoid.

285
00:16:46,910 --> 00:16:50,011
Never draw mass times
acceleration as a force.

286
00:16:50,193 --> 00:16:54,005
For buoyancy, use the fluid's
density, not the block's.

287
00:16:54,187 --> 00:16:57,562
And always ask which side of
the triangle touches the angle.

288
00:16:58,107 --> 00:17:01,413
So mg sin θ isn't a formula to memorise.

289
00:17:01,595 --> 00:17:03,599
It's just one side of a triangle.
